                <p>Account suspension is one of the most frustrating issues in overseas advertising. Incomplete materials and non-standard formats often lead to rejected appeals or repeated supplements, delaying campaign schedules. This article breaks down the appeal process into executable steps and checklists using an operational checklist format, helping you quickly complete material preparation.</p>
<h2>6 Essential Checks Before Filing an Appeal</h2>
<p>Before submitting an appeal, complete the following checks, otherwise even complete materials may be rejected:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Confirm the violation type in the suspension email</strong></li>
<li>Log into Google Ads backend, check specific violation details under "Policies &amp; Settings → Account Status"</li>
<li>Common violation types: trademark abuse, false claims, sensitive category promotion, invalid clicks</li>
<li>
<p>Record the violation code and corresponding policy name for reference in your appeal</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Review recent ad creatives and landing pages</strong></p>
</li>
<li>Export all active ad creatives from the past 30 days</li>
<li>Check if copy contains exaggerated claims, absolute language, or unauthorized brand terms</li>
<li>
<p>Verify landing page functionality: form submissions and payment processes work properly</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Verify account settings information</strong></p>
</li>
<li>Confirm campaign targeting regions match account registration region</li>
<li>Match business license and scope with advertised content</li>
<li>
<p>Update payment method (credit card/corporate account) information</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Collect conversion data and user feedback</strong></p>
</li>
<li>Export GA4 or third-party attribution data proving real conversions from ads</li>
<li>Organize screenshots of resolved user complaints if available</li>
<li>
<p>Prepare explanations if refund rates or return rates are abnormal</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Confirm account historical performance</strong></p>
</li>
<li>Review account violation history and note resolved issues</li>
<li>
<p>Calculate historical spend and CPA data as evidence of campaign quality</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Prepare an emergency backup plan</strong></p>
</li>
<li>Set up campaigns on alternative ad platforms (Meta, TikTok) in advance</li>
<li>Organize existing user data to ensure reachability during suspension</li>
</ol>
<h2>Appeal Materials Checklist: Organized by Violation Type</h2>
<h3>Universal Basic Materials (Required for all appeals)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Business license scan (must be valid)</li>
<li>Front and back photos of legal representative ID</li>
<li>Screenshot of account admin email and account ID</li>
<li>Screenshot of original violation notification email</li>
</ul>
<h3>Materials for Policy Violation Appeals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Exaggerated claims</strong>: Provide product test reports, certifications, user positive feedback screenshots</li>
<li><strong>Trademark infringement</strong>: Submit trademark registration certificate or brand authorization letter</li>
<li><strong>Sensitive industries</strong>: Prepare industry-specific licenses (healthcare, food, finance require special permits)</li>
<li><strong>Click-baiting</strong>: Submit revised ad copy and corresponding landing pages</li>
</ul>
<h3>Materials for Payment or Invalid Traffic Appeals</h3>
<ul>
<li>Third-party payment platform statements proving normal fund flow</li>
<li>Server logs showing real user IP distribution</li>
<li>Backend data screenshots related to suspicious traffic</li>
</ul>
<h3>Materials for Landing Page Issues Appeals</h3>
<ul>
<li>URLs and access credentials for revised landing pages</li>
<li>Website ICP filing screenshot (for China mainland users)</li>
<li>Complete screenshots of product detail pages</li>
</ul>
<h2>Appeal Submission Steps</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Access the Appeal Channel</strong><br />
Log into Google Ads backend, click "Help → Contact Us" in the upper right corner, and select "Account suspended" option. Avoid submitting through regular ticket channels, as they have lower processing priority.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Select Appeal Type</strong><br />
Choose the corresponding appeal type based on the violation description in the email. For first appeals, select "Mistaken suspension"; if you've already fixed issues, select "I've fixed the problem."</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Write the Appeal Statement</strong><br />
The appeal statement is the first impression for reviewers. Recommended structure:<br />
- Paragraph 1: State account ID and violation type clearly<br />
- Paragraph 2: Explain the violation reason (if there's a misunderstanding)<br />
- Paragraph 3: List specific corrective measures taken<br />
- Paragraph 4: Commit to future compliant advertising plans</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Upload Material Attachments</strong><br />
Package all materials into a single PDF or ZIP file. Naming format: AccountID_ViolationType_AppealMaterials. Single file should not exceed 20MB.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5: Track After Submission</strong><br />
Typically receive a response within 1-3 business days after submission. Avoid submitting repeatedly during this period to prevent being marked as harassment.</p>
<h2>3 Tips to Improve Appeal Success Rate</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Proactively acknowledge issues rather than simply denying</strong><br />
Reviewers see many template responses like "Our ads are completely compliant." Acknowledging shortcomings appropriately and showing specific corrective measures leads to higher success rates.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Provide third-party evidence</strong><br />
In addition to internal data, introduce third-party test reports, user review platform data, and industry association certifications to strengthen your case.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Avoid repeatedly appealing the same violation</strong><br />
If your first appeal is rejected, understand the rejection reason before submitting again. Repeated submissions of identical materials will be marked as low priority by the system.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>Quick Checklist Summary</h2>
<p>Before submitting your appeal, do a final check:<br />
- [ ] Carefully read the violation email, recorded violation codes<br />
- [ ] Business license and ID information are clear and readable<br />
- [ ] All ad creatives have been self-checked with noted correction locations<br />
- [ ] Appeal statement is concise with specific corrective measures<br />
- [ ] Material files are named properly without corruption<br />
- [ ] Backup ad platform campaigns are already set up</p>
<p>Account suspension is challenging, but with complete materials and standardized appeal processes, reinstatement is achievable. It's recommended that teams conduct regular creative audits and account health monitoring to prevent violations proactively—prevention is more efficient than post-appeal remediation.</p>
